The presence of this file tells NVIDIA's bootloader package not to flash the
board's boot firmware while it is being installed.

This is NVIDIA's own escape hatch, not a trick. The nvidia-l4t-bootloader
package's maintainer script normally writes the bootloader into the QSPI flash
chip on the Jetson module. That is the right thing to do when you are upgrading
a running Jetson. It is meaningless when you are assembling a filesystem in a
build container: there is no Jetson, and the attempt either fails or, worse,
goes looking at the build machine's own storage.

The check is the first thing the script does. In r39.2 it is line 30 of the
package's postinst -- the file name says "preinstall" but the test lives in the
post-installation script -- and it reads:

    if [ -e "/opt/nvidia/l4t-packages/.nv-l4t-disable-boot-fw-update-in-preinstall" ]; then
        echo "Pre-installing bootloader package, skip flashing"
        exit 0
    fi

Only the file's existence matters; nothing reads what is in it. Writing an
explanation here costs nothing and saves the next reader a search.

Boot firmware for this image is expected to be flashed separately, with NVIDIA's
own tools. See the README.
